---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Waitlist from '../components/Waitlist.astro'
import { LINKS } from '../config'

// The honest door. What Agents As Folders actually means, said plainly,
// before anybody is asked to build or buy anything.
//
// This page teaches the architecture and never shows the instance. That is
// the standing rule and it is not a style choice: the patterns are the
// lesson, the running system is only the evidence they work. No screenshots,
// no names, no identifiers, nothing that maps to a real host.

const jobs = [
  {
    job: 'Configuration',
    plain: 'Every agent has 1 role and the approved tools for that role. Nothing else.',
  },
  {
    job: 'Access',
    plain: 'Getting in is checked against who you are, not a password everybody shares.',
  },
  {
    job: 'Change control',
    plain: 'Changes get planned and checked before they apply, and they can be undone.',
  },
  {
    job: 'Monitoring',
    plain: 'Somebody watches whether the thing is alive and doing its job.',
  },
  {
    job: 'Recovery',
    plain: 'The state is backed up, so a bad day is a bad hour instead.',
  },
  {
    job: 'Drift',
    plain: 'What you believe is running gets compared to what is actually running.',
  },
]

const given = [
  'its own place to run',
  'its own workspace',
  'its own stored state',
  'its own identity on the network',
  'its own limits',
  'its own access policy',
]
---

<Base
  title="What this actually is"
  description="Agents As Folders is not a slogan about folders. It is Hermes agents running on ICM brains, doing real work in a business, inside a layer that decides what they are allowed to touch."
  path="/what-this-is/"
>
  <section style="padding-bottom:1rem">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <span class="badge badge--accent">The whole idea, said once</span>
      <h1>What this actually is</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The name is not a slogan about folders. It is the literal shape of the
        thing: agents, built as folders.
      </p>
      <p style="color:var(--text)">
        Most pages like this one open by selling you something. This one just
        says what the thing is, because if it is not for you, you should be
        able to work that out in 2 minutes and leave.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>The stack, bottom to top</h2>
      <pre class="tree"><b>4. The surfaces</b>   <i>email, community, social, wherever signal arrives</i>
<b>3. The agents</b>     <i>Hermes agents running against those brains</i>
<b>2. The ICM brain</b>  <i>numbered folders, 1 job each, on top of that memory</i>
<b>1. Hermes</b>         <i>the agent. It remembers, and it learns</i></pre>
      <p>
        Hermes remembers. The folders organize. The agent walks the folders and
        does the work.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Recall is not structure.</strong> Memory gives you back what
          was said. Structure gives an agent somewhere to stand and a job to do
          when it stands there. Most people stop at recall, and then wonder why
          the agent still sounds like it just met them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>The part almost nobody talks about</h2>
      <p>
        Here is the question business owners actually ask, and it is never the
        one that gets answered.
      </p>
      <p style="color:var(--text)">
        They are not worried the agent cannot do the work. They are worried
        about what it does at 3 in the morning when nobody is watching.
      </p>
      <p>
        The usual answer is some version of trust it. Write a careful
        instruction, tell the agent to ask before it sends anything, and hope
        it holds.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is a promise, not a boundary. It lives in the same place as
        everything else the agent might get talked out of, and the agent is the
        one enforcing it against itself.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>The honest answer is that it does not get to decide.</strong>
          What it can reach, what it can spend, and where it can go are handed
          to it by a layer above, and it has no way to widen them. Not because
          it promised. Because it cannot.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>The 6 jobs that layer does</h3>
      <p>
        None of this is new. It is how every serious company already runs its
        own systems. The only new part is pointing it at an agent.
      </p>
      <div class="scroller">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>The job</th><th>Said plainly</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {jobs.map((j) => (
              <tr>
                <td style="white-space:nowrap;color:var(--accent)">{j.job}</td>
                <td>{j.plain}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The 6th one is the sleeper. Everybody understands backups. Almost
        nobody thinks about the gap between what they believe is set up and
        what is really running. Every owner has lived that already, just with
        people instead of software: the process everyone agreed to and nobody
        follows.
      </p>

      <h3>What each agent is handed</h3>
      <p>From outside itself, where it cannot argue with any of it:</p>
      <ul>
        {given.map((g) => <li>{g}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p>
        Separate, but still part of 1 system somebody can see across. Not 1
        assistant that can reach everything, and not 6 disconnected tools that
        nobody is watching.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Do I run this, or am I just describing it</h2>
      <p>
        I run it. Agents with separate jobs and separate memory. Work that
        happens on a schedule whether or not I am at the desk. A morning packet
        of what needs deciding. Files that show the state of the work instead
        of a chat log I have to scroll. Proof when something publishes, so I am
        not taking the agent's word for it.
      </p>
      <p>
        It runs Ryan McKinney's BuildMarketClose framework. That is his
        process, not mine. What is mine is the automation on top of it.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>You will not see it on this site.</strong> The architecture
          is the lesson and I will teach all of it. The running system stays
          off camera, because it has other people's work inside it and they did
          not sign up to be content. Anything I demonstrate is built for the
          demonstration.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p style="color:var(--muted)">
        That is a real limit and it cuts against me, so it is worth saying out
        loud rather than letting you notice it later. It means you get the
        pattern and the reasoning, and you take my word for the rest.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Who this is for</h2>
      <p>
        A business owner who already tried the note taking app, already has an
        AI subscription, and is tired of explaining the same business every
        morning. Someone who wants the work to happen while they sleep and
        wants to know exactly what it was allowed to touch while it did.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is not for someone who wants a chatbot that sounds clever. That is a
        real thing to want. It is just not this.
      </p>

      <h2>Where to start</h2>
      <p>
        At the bottom. 3 folders, tonight, with nothing installed. The whole
        stack above is built out of that 1 move repeated, and if the first step
        does nothing for you the rest will not either.
      </p>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn" href="/start">Build 3 folders</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/icm">What ICM is</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/hermes">What Hermes is</a>
      </div>

      <hr />

      <p>
        ICM was named by <strong>Jake van Clief</strong>. The paper is
        <a href={LINKS.icmPaper}>here</a> and the reference build is
        <a href={LINKS.icmRepo}>here</a>. I did not invent it. I use it, I
        teach it, and I build inside it.
      </p>

      <h2>When the classroom opens</h2>
      <p>
        My VIP classroom is where the finished builds go. It is not open yet
        and there is nothing to buy today. Leave your email and you will hear
        before anyone else.
      </p>
      <Waitlist id="thesis-waitlist" />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
